When one dump is enough ✅
==============================
* True monitor deadlock (synchronized)
* jstack will usually print an explicit section:
    Found one Java-level deadlock: (banner)
* Obvious lock cycle visible in the text
    Even without the banner, you might clearly see:
    - Thread A: BLOCKED → waiting to lock <0x123> which is locked by Thread B
    - Thread B: BLOCKED → waiting to lock <0x456> which is locked by Thread A
    That’s also definitive from one dump.
===============================
    Thread A:
      owns  → Monitor M2 (<0x456>)
      waits → Monitor M1 (<0x123>)  ← owned by Thread B

    Thread B:
      owns  → Monitor M1 (<0x123>)
      waits → Monitor M2 (<0x456>)  ← owned by Thread A

    This cycle is the deadlock.
          ┌─────────────────────────┐
          │  Monitor M1  (<0x123>)  │
          │-------------------------│
          │ Owner     : Thread B    │
          │ Entry Set : Thread A    │  ← BLOCKED trying to enter
          │ Wait Set  : (empty)     │
          └─────────────────────────┘
                      ▲
                      │
                      │  Thread A wants M1
                      │
          ┌─────────────────────────┐
          │  Monitor M2  (<0x456>)   │
          │-------------------------│
          │ Owner     : Thread A    │
          │ Entry Set : Thread B    │  ← BLOCKED trying to enter
          │ Wait Set  : (empty)     │
          └─────────────────────────┘
                      ▲
                      │
                      │  Thread B wants M2
                      │
===================

When one dump is not enough ⚠️
------------------------------------

It’s not a deadlock; it’s just “blocked right now”
A single snapshot can show threads BLOCKED, but they might unblock 2 ms later. You can’t know from one snapshot whether it’s permanent.

Deadlock involving java.util.concurrent locks
With ReentrantLock / AbstractQueuedSynchronizer, jstack may show:

WAITING (parking)

frames like Unsafe.park, LockSupport.park, AQS.acquire
That could be:

normal contention,

long wait,

or an actual deadlock cycle.
One dump often can’t prove it.

Distributed “deadlock”
If threads are waiting on:

network I/O,

DB calls,

Kafka,

remote services,
it can look like a deadlock but isn’t a JVM lock deadlock.

The practical rule

If jstack prints “Found one Java-level deadlock” → one dump is enough.

Otherwise, one dump only tells you the current state, not whether it will resolve.

That’s why people take 2–3 dumps spaced out: persistence across time is the giveaway.

What multiple dumps proves

If the same threads show the same:

state (BLOCKED / WAITING (parking))

“waiting on …”

same lock addresses / same owner threads
across all dumps → it’s effectively stuck (deadlock or permanent contention).

